As a young man, Augustine prayed to be made pure and added two words: "not yet." He was afraid, he admitted, that God would answer too soon. He stole pears he did not want, lied to his mother on the shore at Carthage and sailed away while she prayed, and spent nine years in a sect. Then a child's voice in a garden in Milan chanted "Take up and read." The restless teacher became a priest seized by a crowd while he wept, a bishop with a verse against gossip cut into his table, and the author of the Confessions. How does a man who fears his own prayer become one of the great teachers of the Church?
